C = [
("h1", "9. 1 Corinthians 10:23–33: The Market, the Feast and the Glory of God"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Corinthians 10:23–33 gives the Corinthian a practical course for meat in the market and at a feast in an unbeliever’s house. It allows what chapter 8 and chapter 10 did not forbid, it limits it by another man’s conscience, and it ends with two sentences, “do all to the glory of God” and “give none offence,” which sum up the course and do not replace the steps."),
("sub", "9.1 All things lawful, not all expedient"),
("p", "“All things are lawful for me, but all things are not expedient: all things are lawful for me, but all things edify not” (1 Corinthians 10:23). The sentence repeats 6:12 and adds a clause. Chapter 14 reads it. Here it opens the course: what is lawful is to be weighed by whether it is expedient and whether it edifies. “Let no man seek his own, but every man another’s wealth” (1 Corinthians 10:24). The word “wealth” in the KJV is used in its old sense of welfare. The ground is the same as Romans 15:2: the neighbour’s good."),
("sub", "9.2 The market"),
("p", "“Whatsoever is sold in the shambles, that eat, asking no question for conscience sake: for the earth is the Lord’s, and the fulness thereof” (1 Corinthians 10:25, 26). The believer is told to eat what is sold in the market and not to ask. The ground is Scripture: “the earth is the Lord’s, and the fulness thereof.” The question he is not to ask is the question of origin, and Paul says it is not asked “for conscience sake.” Constable reads this as practical wisdom, “It was wise not to ask if someone had offered the meat to an idol.” The volume agrees with the reading and notes that Paul does not call it wise. He says it is the believer’s liberty, and he gives the earth’s ownership as the reason."),
("sub", "9.3 The feast"),
("p", "“If any of them that believe not bid you to a feast, and ye be disposed to go; whatsoever is set before you, eat, asking no question for conscience sake” (1 Corinthians 10:27). The believer may accept an unbeliever’s invitation. He may eat what is set before him, and he is again not to ask. The invitation is to a feast in a house, not in a temple, and Paul has already forbidden the table of devils (10:21). The volume notes that Paul can call both things by the same word, “eat,” and tell the same believer to flee the one and accept the other. The difference is the setting and the ground."),
("sub", "9.4 When someone says it was sacrificed"),
("p", "“But if any man say unto you, This is offered in sacrifice unto idols, eat not for his sake that shewed it, and for conscience sake: for the earth is the Lord’s, and the fulness thereof” (1 Corinthians 10:28). The limit comes when someone says so. The case is not stated. Paul does not say who the “man” is, whether a believer, a host or a pagan. The next verse names whose conscience is meant. “Conscience, I say, not thine own, but of the other: for why is my liberty judged of another man’s conscience?” (1 Corinthians 10:29). The conscience is not the eater’s. It is the other man’s, and the eater is to give way for the sake of it. The eater’s liberty stands; it is not abolished by being limited."),
("p", "Paul puts a question that may be read two ways. “Why is my liberty judged of another man’s conscience?” (1 Corinthians 10:29) and “For if I by grace be a partaker, why am I evil spoken of for that for which I give thanks?” (1 Corinthians 10:30). The volume reads them as the strong man’s protest, which Paul states and does not answer by denying it. The answer is in verse 31, and it is a different kind of answer, not a rule about protests. The strong man’s liberty is real; the course is to do all to God’s glory and to give no offence."),
("sub", "9.5 The glory of God and the offence"),
("p", "“Whether therefore ye eat, or drink, or whatsoever ye do, do all to the glory of God. Give none offence, neither to the Jews, nor to the Gentiles, nor to the church of God: even as I please all men in all things, not seeking mine own profit, but the profit of many, that they may be saved” (1 Corinthians 10:31–33). Constable says, “What glorifies God is what puts His preferences, plans, and program first (cf. Colossians 3:17).” The volume agrees that the glory of God is the end of the believer’s conduct. It does not agree that the sentence functions as a single rule from which every case can be answered, since the question of the market, the feast and the one who says “this is offered” has been answered by Paul in verses 25 to 29 by particulars. The sentence of verse 31 sums up what the particulars serve. The three groups of verse 32 are read in Study 36, and the volume cites the verse and says no more about them."),
("p", "The offence of verse 32 is the stumbling of chapter 8 stated for the three groups. The ground of “give none offence” is stated in verse 33: Paul pleases all men in all things “not seeking mine own profit, but the profit of many, that they may be saved.” The end he names is the salvation of many. The verse recalls 9:19–22, and the volume reads the two together as Paul’s own conduct. The chapter that began with “flee from idolatry” ends with a man who pleases others for their good, and the two are not in conflict."),
("ctbl", "1 Corinthians 10:23–33 by the five questions.", [
    ("Question", "Answer from the passage", "Verse"),
    ("Does Paul command?", "He gives a course: eat what is sold, eat what is set before you, do not ask; eat not if told", "10:25, 27, 28"),
    ("What limit?", "Eat not for the sake of him that shewed it; give none offence", "10:28, 32"),
    ("To whom is it owed?", "To the other man’s conscience, and to Jews, Gentiles and the church", "10:29, 32"),
    ("What ground?", "The earth is the Lord’s; the neighbour’s wealth; the glory of God; the profit of many", "10:24, 26, 31, 33"),
    ("What is unsaid?", "Who the “man” of verse 28 is; what “offence” is to each group", "Chapter 16"),
  ], [0.26, 0.52, 0.22]),
("sub", "9.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "Paul allows the market and the feast, and tells the believer not to ask, for conscience sake and because the earth is the Lord’s.",
    "He limits the course by another man’s conscience, when someone says the meat was offered, and the eater’s liberty stands.",
    "“Do all to the glory of God” sums up the particulars and does not replace them.",
    "The three groups of verse 32 are left to Study 36.",
  ]),
]
